# Borrow checking via QTT quantities (Issue #122) — positive case.
#
# A unique borrow of a resource: `withBorrow r k` hands `k` the *only*
# borrow of `r`; `r` itself is consumed (linear), so the owner cannot touch
# it while the borrow is live. The borrow is threaded through the read/write
# operations in continuation-passing style so that *every* borrow is a
# lambda binder — the in-judgment quantity enforcement (Issue #66) then
# fires at every step: a borrow used twice or dropped is an elaboration
# error (see 21a/21b/21c-*-neg.ep). Unannotated lambdas inherit the
# quantity from the expected Pi (Idris 2 behaviour), so `lam (b2 :
# Borrow). ...` against `Pi (1 b2 : Borrow). Nat` is linear-checked.
#
# `Borrow` is deliberately *not* indexed by the resource: the owner index
# would have to travel as an implicit argument, and the elaborated
# implicit would count as an extra term-position use of the linear owner,
# defeating the count. The borrow is a capability token; `withBorrow`'s
# type is the sole link between resource and borrow, which is all the
# enforced discipline needs (exclusive access comes from linearity, not
# from the index).
#
# The operations are postulates (mock backend), exactly as in the session
# demos: the types enforce the borrow discipline; the embedded demo
# (examples/embedded/qtt_borrow.nim) binds the same op sequence to a real
# buffer, the Wu & Xi "typed API over untrusted backend" architecture.
# import nat

postulate Res : ?
postulate Borrow : ?

postulate allocRes : Pi (n : Nat). Res

# Scoped unique borrow (bracket): k gets the borrow and computes a Nat;
# when k finishes, the backend releases the resource. The linear `r` is
# fully consumed, so a well-typed client cannot leak it — the scope owns
# the lifetime, exactly like `withFile` / `bracket` in ordinary languages.
postulate withBorrow : Pi (1 r : Res). Pi (k : Pi (1 b : Borrow). Nat). Nat

postulate readB : Pi (1 b : Borrow).
  Pi (k : Pi (v : Nat). Pi (1 b2 : Borrow). Nat). Nat
postulate writeB : Pi (1 b : Borrow). Pi (v : Nat).
  Pi (k : Pi (1 b2 : Borrow). Nat). Nat
postulate endB : Pi (1 b : Borrow). Pi (v : Nat). Nat

# Well-bracketed client: read the head cell, write back its successor,
# read again, finish the borrow with the new value. Every borrow binder
# is used exactly once; the owner `r` is mentioned only in type positions
# inside the scope (a term mention would be a second use). The borrow
# cannot escape: `k` returns `Nat`, so there is no channel by which the
# borrow could outlive the scope.
proc borrowClient (1 r : Res) : Nat =
  withBorrow r (lam (b : Borrow).
    readB b (lam (v : Nat). lam (b2 : Borrow).
      writeB b2 (suc v) (lam (b3 : Borrow).
        readB b3 (lam (v2 : Nat). lam (b4 : Borrow).
          endB b4 v2))))
